00:11:15,120 --> 00:11:27,360
Dean, the reason why that you're not finding a lot of information on the web about this is that most holograms need a recording media in order to work, whether it would be a holographic plate or holographic film.

89
00:11:27,360 --> 00:11:36,880
Now, granted, it is possible to shoot a hologram where you have parts of the object actually projecting out in space toward the viewer.

90
00:11:36,880 --> 00:11:48,000
And if a hologram is set up properly, you may even be able to shoot holograms where you have an object itself, which seemingly floats in space in front of that plate.

91
00:11:48,000 --> 00:12:02,400
But the idea of creating an object that just hangs in space is something that's just not possible to do at this point through holography.

92
00:12:02,960 --> 00:12:09,920
The laws of physics themselves state that any time that you're using light, something has to be there to stop it.

93
00:12:09,920 --> 00:12:22,560
And to take a laser beam and send it through the air and somehow draw out whatever it is that you're uh looking to see three dimensionally doesn't work very well because there's nothing there to stop the beam.

94
00:12:22,560 --> 00:13:16,360
Now, on some of the laser light shows, and I believe this is where some people get some of the misinformation, they'll use either some steam or what I've seen at several laser light shows they'll use a fine mist of falling water to pass that laser beam through and this happens to when the laser beam goes through it to draw whatever image it is it happens to light up that water or that steam but it's not a completely three dimensional image even though you see it rotating it's rotating because the computer program is telling it to appear to be turning but if you have a wall of water or steam that's showing you this image there's nothing in that image which is going to be closer to you or further away from you like in a hologram.

95
00:13:16,360 --> 00:14:00,200
